Review BOTH exact final summary snapshots below against literature through 2026-10-20 UTC. This validates revised SLE wording and a newly discovered calciphylaxis finding. Return separate verdict consistent_with_caveats/needs_revision/insufficient_evidence, actual major/minor errors, corrections, primary sources and actual queries/coverage/access limits. Separate safeguards already satisfied from unresolved errors. SLE explicitly distinguishes anti-C1q DOI 10.1371/journal.ppat.1014666 from a separate Z-DNA preprint, retains mouse/strain/stage limits and null proteinuria. Calciphylaxis DOI 10.1016/j.jacadv.2026.103205: 30 matched case-control pairs, 70-person cohort, 67% ESKD, small industry-funded observational data, no PPi intervention, no pain association. Assess adding PPi associations without causation/benefit. Search qualifying/null/contradictory evidence and verify historical therapy caveats. Bounded selected-report local maps, no publication endorsement. No library changes.

{"candidateId":"sle-summary-candidate-904291fc29fb","contentDigest":"f9be34698291844f0295d2c302b4e4ca1bfeee108133e1108df67c245713f940","summaryRevision":"summary-904291fc29fb","scope":"A bounded nucleic-acid, interferon and B-cell circuit with a renal injury branch.","paragraphs":["This bounded SLE model connects self-reactive antibodies and nucleic-acid-containing immune complexes with innate sensing and B-cell responses. In the cited culture systems, RNA- and DNA-containing complexes stimulate TLR7- or TLR9-dependent plasmacytoid dendritic-cell interferon production. Type I interferon can increase monocyte BAFF release and promote stages of B-cell differentiation; BAFF and enhanced TLR7 signalling support B-cell survival in the cited mouse models. Autoantibody-producing cells and immune complexes provide a proposed route connecting these processes. A newly added anti-C1q mouse study, distinct from the separate Z-DNA/curli preprint, shows that bacterial biofilm curli/DNA exposure can increase anti-C1q and selected anti-dsDNA responses, adding an experimental upstream route into autoantibody production. The existing renal branch places deposited immune complexes upstream of Fc-receptor-dependent inflammation in lupus-prone mice.","The evidence spans human observations, cell cultures and animal models; it does not establish a universal, self-sufficient causal circuit for every SLE phenotype. Curli effects depended on mouse strain and disease stage: already-high responses in late-disease NZBxW/F1 mice did not increase further, and proteinuria did not significantly increase in either lupus-prone model at the measured timepoints. The biofilm experiment does not establish infection as a cause of human SLE or prove worsening nephritis. Reduced apoptotic clearance remains a proposed initiating route, TLR9 can have different effects across models, and renal immune-complex deposition alone did not produce severe nephritis in an Fc-receptor-deficient model. The cited belimumab and anifrolumab trials support selected clinical outcomes and populations, while anifrolumab and enpatoran programmes include negative primary endpoints. Target engagement, an approved indication and benefit across all manifestations remain distinct conclusions."]}
{"candidateId":"calciphylaxis-summary-candidate-d5f1a8f06f12","contentDigest":"d0819a6282a2c4385789b82f2975a0ee36471893d30e42b43231ea643fd145e3","summaryRevision":"summary-d5f1a8f06f12","scope":"A bounded map of calcific uraemic arteriolopathy: mineral-metabolism drivers, calcification-inhibitor deficiency (MGP/vitamin K, fetuin-A), vascular smooth-muscle osteogenic change, arteriolar calcification, thrombosis and skin ischaemic necrosis, plus curated therapies.","paragraphs":["This bounded calciphylaxis map centres on small-vessel calcification together with thrombosis and vascular narrowing in predominantly dialysis-related disease. Human studies associate disease with aspects of mineral metabolism, vitamin K antagonism and reduced calcification-inhibitor activity; cultured smooth-muscle-cell and animal experiments support mechanisms involving phosphate, matrix Gla protein and fetuin-A. Human skin studies link an osteogenic programme with arteriolar mineral deposition and show thrombosis/intimal hyperplasia in lesional skin. Endothelial culture experiments add an inflammatory tissue-factor route to coagulation. The resulting model proposes convergence of calcification and vascular occlusion rather than a sufficient explanation based on mineral deposition alone. A newly added human registry study found lower circulating pyrophosphate in 30 calciphylaxis cases than in 30 matched controls; in its 70-patient cohort, lower levels were also associated with higher short-term mortality. This adds an observational calcification-inhibitor signal without demonstrating that PPi deficiency causes calciphylaxis.","Risk-factor findings vary across cohorts: phosphate and parathyroid-hormone results are not uniform, and vascular calcification can occur in clinically normal skin. Observational associations and experimental vascular calcification should not be equated with proven human calciphylaxis causation. Treatment signals are also limited: CALCIPHYX did not meet its wound or pain primary endpoints, uncontrolled sodium-thiosulfate improvement reports do not establish efficacy, and the cited vitamin K trial improved a biomarker without reducing coronary calcification progression. A secondary cinacalcet prevention analysis does not establish treatment of existing lesions. The pyrophosphate study was small, industry-funded and observational, with substantial within-person variability; PPi did not correlate with pain intensity. Raising PPi was not tested, and no treatment benefit is inferred. This is a selected-report local candidate with pending domain review."]}
